\section{Enzyme types (biological)}{
Enzymes can be classified by their biological rules.
Three types of enzymes can be represented: glycosyltransferases (GTs),
glycoside hydrolases (GHs), and sulfotransferases (STs).
\itemize{
\item GTs catalyze the transfer of a sugar residue from a donor to an acceptor, thus building up glycan structures.
\item GHs catalyze the removal of a sugar residue from a substrate, thus breaking down glycan structures.
\item STs add a sulfate substituent to an existing glycan residue.
}

One special subtype of GTs are starter GTs (or initiating GTs),
which catalyze the addition of the first sugar residue onto a non-glycan substrate, thus initiating glycosylation.
}

\section{Enzyme types (computational)}{
There're also three types of enzymes you'll encounter in this package depending on how we define them,
they are concrete enzymes, abstract enzymes, and virtual enzymes.

Concrete enzymes are the enzymes represented by gene symbols, e.g. B4GALT1.
There can be many isoenzymes on this level.
For example, B4GALT1/2/3/4/5 are all isoenzymes with similar substrate specificity.

Abstract enzymes are conventional enzymes without considering isoenzymes.
For example, the above enzymes are described as b4GalT.
Other examples include ManI, ManII, GnTI, etc.
Currently only abstract enzymes for N-glycan biosynthesis are supported.

Both concrete and abstract enzymes can be used in \code{enzyme} or \code{enzymes} arguments throughout this package.
They are also supported by the \code{enzyme()} function.

Another type of enzymes is called the virtual enzymes.
You'll see them in the results of \code{\link[=path_biosynthesis_virtual]{path_biosynthesis_virtual()}} and \code{\link[=trace_biosynthesis_virtual]{trace_biosynthesis_virtual()}}.
Find out more in the documentation of these two functions.
}

\section{Explanation about \code{glyenzy_enzyme}}{
An \code{enzyme()} is a list with the following elements:
\enumerate{
\item \code{name}: the name of the enzyme, usually the gene symbol.
\item \code{rules}: a list of \code{glyenzy_enzyme_rule} objects.
Each rule is a list with the following fields:
\itemize{
\item \code{acceptor}: the motif that the enzyme recognizes
\item \code{acceptor_alignment}: the alignment of the \code{acceptor}
\item \code{rejects}: the motifs that the enzyme rejects to act on
\item \code{requires}: optional OR-valued context motifs. When present, at least
one requirement must match.
\item \code{product}: the product generated by the enzyme
\item \code{acceptor_idx}: the node index of the acceptor where the enzyme acts on.
For GTs, this is the node new residue is attached to.
For GHs, this is the node that is removed.
For starter GTs, \code{acceptor_idx} is 0.
\item \code{product_idx}: the node index of the product residue in the product structure.
For GTs, this is the index of the newly added residue in the product.
For STs, this is the product node corresponding to the modified
acceptor residue. For GHs, this is \code{NULL}.
\item \code{new_residue}: the new residue added by a GT. For GHs and STs, this is
\code{NULL}.
\item \code{new_linkage}: the linkage of the new residue added by a GT. For GHs
and STs, this is \code{NULL}.
\item \code{new_substituent}: for STs, the sulfate token added by the rule, such
as \code{"6S"}. This field is absent for other enzyme types.
\item \code{product_sub}: for STs, the canonical complete substituent state of the
modified product residue, such as \code{"3S,6S"}. This field is absent for
other enzyme types.
}
\item \code{type}: the broad type of the enzyme, \code{"GT"} for glycosyltransferase,
\code{"GH"} for glycoside hydrolase, or \code{"ST"} for sulfotransferase.
Starter GTs are encoded as \code{type = "GT"}.
\item \code{species}: the species of the enzyme, e.g. "human" or "mouse".
\item \code{glycan_type}: \code{NULL} for enzymes that can act on all glycan classes, or
one or more of \code{"N"}, \code{"O"}, \code{"lipid"}, and \code{"free"} for
class-specific enzymes. Structures ending in Glc or Gal without a
represented carrier are compatible with both \code{"lipid"} and \code{"free"}.
}

You can see all these information by printing the enzyme object.
}

